---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { getPost } from "../../data/journal";
import { site } from "../../config";

const post = getPost("count-is-not-rhythm")!;
---

<Layout
  title={`${post.title} | EyesRhythm`}
  description={post.description}
  type="article"
  published={post.date}
  image={post.cover}
  imageAlt={post.coverAlt}
>
  <main id="main">
    <header class="cover">
      <img
        src={post.cover}
        alt={post.coverAlt}
        width="1280"
        height="720"
      />
      <div class="cover__shade"></div>
      <div class="cover__copy">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>{post.kicker}</p>
        <h1 class="display">{post.title}</h1>
        <p class="lede">{post.description}</p>
        <p class="meta">{post.read} · 7 September 2026</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="wrap article">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          When you look at the small EyesRhythm window, you see a number,
          and it isn’t how many times you blinked today or a streak you
          could collect. It’s a picture of this stretch of work, showing
          how often you blinked and whether those blinks were spread out
          or bunched up.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most blink apps only count, which sounds useful until you
          remember that your eyes don’t live on a total. They live on
          the gaps between blinks, which is when the surface starts to
          dry. A
          <a href="/blink-rate-detector">blink rate detector</a>
          has to look at both.
        </p>
        <p>
          We already wrote about
          <a href="/journal/screens-cut-blink-rate">why screens make you
          blink less</a>. This one is about the number next to your work,
          and how to read it without turning it into another job.
        </p>

        <h2>The same six blinks, two different minutes</h2>
        <p>
          Picture a minute with six blinks in it, which is already a low
          rate, the kind researchers saw when office workers looked at a
          screen instead of sitting quietly.
          <sup><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>
          Those same six blinks can still mean two different minutes.
        </p>
        <p>
          If they are spread out, you wait about twelve seconds between
          each one, and the eye gets a little dry before a blink wets it
          again. If they come in two clumps, with a long empty stretch in
          the middle, more than half the minute can go by with no blink
          at all. The count is the same, but the drying is much worse,
          and a 2023 paper drew that picture with the same average and
          very different uncovered time.
          <sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>
        </p>

        <figure class="tracks" aria-label="Even blinks versus clustered blinks over one minute">
          <p class="tracks__title">Same six blinks. Different minute.</p>
          <div class="track">
            <p class="track__label">Even</p>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 600 36" role="img" aria-hidden="true">
              <line x1="8" y1="18" x2="592" y2="18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"></line>
              <circle cx="8" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
              <circle cx="124" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
              <circle cx="240" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
              <circle cx="356" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
              <circle cx="472" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
              <circle cx="592" cy="18" r="6" fill="#0071e3"></circle>
            </svg>
            <p class="track__note">A blink about every 12 seconds</p>
          </div>
          <div class="track">
            <p class="track__label">Lumpy</p>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 600 36" role="img" aria-hidden="true">
              <line x1="8" y1="18" x2="592" y2="18" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"></line>
              <circle cx="8" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
              <circle cx="68" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
              <circle cx="98" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
              <circle cx="512" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
              <circle cx="542" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
              <circle cx="592" cy="18" r="6" fill="#e07a72"></circle>
            </svg>
            <p class="track__note">A cluster, a long wait, another cluster</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            After Cardona, Argilés and Pérez-Cabré, 2023. The average
            looks the same, and the long gap is where the eye dries.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “A complete characterization of blink parameters is relevant to
            better understand ocular dryness related to surface exposure and
            to advice patients towards a reeducation of their blinking
            habits.”
          </p>
          <cite>Cardona, Argilés and Pérez-Cabré, 2023<sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          What they mean is that the count is only part of the story, and
          that people can relearn a better blink habit. How often you
          blink is the rate, and how steady those blinks are is the
          regularity. You need both, because slow and even still leaves
          the eye dry, and fast but bunched still leaves a long dry wait.
        </p>

        <h2>Two questions, one score</h2>
        <p>
          EyesRhythm puts those two things into one number, the
          <strong>Ocular Rhythm Index</strong>, or ORI, which runs from
          0 to 100 and sits in the window.
        </p>
        <p>
          How often still matters more, because a blink that never comes
          cannot be even. How steady still matters too, so a lumpy minute
          doesn’t look fine just because the count added up.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app is built for desk work, not for talking and not for
          sleep, since people blink more when they talk and less when
          they stare at a screen.
          <sup><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>
          EyesRhythm aims at a comfortable screen pace, about 15 to 20
          full blinks a minute, spaced out enough that the eye stays wet.
        </p>

        <figure class="rates" aria-label="How to glance at ORI">
          <div class="rate">
            <p class="rate__n">~18</p>
            <p class="rate__unit">blinks / min</p>
            <p class="rate__label">A calm minute at the desk</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rate">
            <p class="rate__n">0–100</p>
            <p class="rate__unit">ORI</p>
            <p class="rate__label">How often and how even</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rate rate--low">
            <p class="rate__n">50</p>
            <p class="rate__unit">default nudge</p>
            <p class="rate__label">When the score has been low</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            About 15 to 20 blinks a minute is a healthy pace at a screen,
            and ORI is that rate plus how even the gaps were.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Regularity is simply whether the waits between blinks are
          similar or all over the place.
          <sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>
          Even waits score high, while a clump followed by a long wait
          scores low. EyesRhythm looks at your recent blinks this way,
          not as a medical test, but as a live picture of this stretch of
          looking.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/ripples.jpg"
            alt="Even concentric ripples on still water, spreading from a single point of light."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Regularity is how even the gaps are, so the count can look
            fine while the waits are all over the place.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>How to read the window</h2>
        <p>
          You don’t have to watch the number, because the app is meant to
          sit beside your work. When you do look, this is enough:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>High ORI.</strong> You have enough blinks, and they
            are not arriving in panics and silences, so this minute is
            going okay.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Around the middle.</strong> This is where the default
            nudge lives, when you are blinking less or the gaps have gone
            lumpy, or both. A full blink, a look away, and then back to
            the page is often all you need.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Low ORI.</strong> The waits have gotten long, or
            there are too few blinks, and when the letters go soft this
            is usually why, not the screen.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The score only runs while the camera can see both eyes looking
          at the screen, so if you look down, turn to a side monitor, or
          leave the chair, it pauses on purpose. That isn’t a bug,
          because it’s only counting screen time, and the
          <a href="/journal/camera-setup">camera setup</a> guide is the
          short version of sitting so those minutes count.
        </p>
        <p>
          Comfortable blinking isn’t a perfect beat, and the waits can
          vary a little. Sore, dry eyes often blink more, with shorter
          gaps, as if the eye is asking for another wipe.
          <sup><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>
          A good score isn’t a locked 100 so much as enough blinks
          without being chaotic, and the number will move, which is
          normal.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            “The tendency towards decreased blink rate during visual function
            tasks, such as computer use, reading, video gaming, and watching
            TV can exacerbate the signs and symptoms of dry eye.”
          </p>
          <cite>Johnston, Rodriguez, Lane, Ousler and Abelson, 2013<sup><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup></cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Staring at a screen, a page, a game, or the TV often means
          fewer blinks, and dry, tired eyes get worse when that goes on.
        </p>

        <h2>A two-minute practice</h2>
        <p>
          Nobody decides to blink less, and focus does it for you. The
          way back isn’t a lecture so much as a few minutes of even
          blinking, so your eyelids remember when work gets intense.
          <sup><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>
        </p>
        <p>
          Practice is a two-minute coach where a ball moves and a quiet
          beep comes about every three seconds, which is close to twenty
          blinks a minute. Follow the ball with your eyes, blink on the
          beep, and keep your head still. Let the lids actually meet,
          because a half blink doesn’t wet the eye.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then close it and go back to work. You don’t need to watch
          yourself, because a little practice is enough for the live
          score to have something to climb toward.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/dewdrop.jpg"
            alt="A round dewdrop on a sage leaf, catching blue sky like a tiny lens."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Blinking keeps the eye wet, and even blinking is what a long
            screen day often forgets.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>What this number is not</h2>
        <p>
          ORI isn’t a diagnosis or a grade. Bad light, glasses, a camera
          that cannot see your eyes, or a meeting already using the
          webcam can all move the number, because the app is a desk tool,
          not a clinic.
        </p>
        <p>
          A low stretch doesn’t mean something is wrong with you. If
          your eyes stay sore, or your vision changes, see an eye doctor
          rather than asking a window on your laptop.
        </p>
        <p>
          Leave the window where you can see it if you want the nudge,
          or tuck it away and let the quiet pulse do the work. Either
          way, what matters is this minute, not a count on a sticky note.
        </p>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ol class="sources" id="sources">
          <li id="ref-1">
            Tsubota K, Nakamori K. Dry eyes and video display terminals.
            <cite>New England Journal of Medicine</cite>. 1993;328:584.
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM199302253280817">doi:10.1056/NEJM199302253280817</a>
          </li>
          <li id="ref-2">
            Cardona G, Argilés M, Pérez-Cabré E. Loss of blink regularity
            and its impact on ocular surface exposure.
            <cite>Diagnostics</cite>. 2023;13:2362.
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics13142362">doi:10.3390/diagnostics13142362</a>
          </li>
          <li id="ref-3">
            Johnston PR, Rodriguez J, Lane KJ, Ousler G, Abelson MB. The
            interblink interval in normal and dry eye subjects.
            <cite>Clinical Ophthalmology</cite>. 2013;7:253–259.
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.2147/OPTH.S39104">doi:10.2147/OPTH.S39104</a>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="note">
          EyesRhythm is a wellness companion, not a medical device. It is
          not intended to diagnose, treat, or prevent disease. Persistent
          discomfort or a change in vision belongs with a licensed eye-care
          professional.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="back">
        <a href="/journal">All journal</a>
        ·
        <a href="/journal/screens-cut-blink-rate">Why screens cut blink rate</a>
        ·
        <a href="/journal/camera-setup">Camera setup</a>
        ·
        <button type="button" data-open-purchase>{site.cta}</button>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .cover {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: min(72vh, 640px);
    display: grid;
    align-items: end;
    color: #fff;
    background: #1d1d1f;
  }

  .cover img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .cover__shade {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15) 20%,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.62) 100%
    );
  }

  .cover__copy {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: min(var(--wrap), calc(100% - var(--gutter) * 2));
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 48px 0 40px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .cover .kicker {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86);
  }

  .cover .display {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.2rem);
    max-width: 16ch;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .cover .lede {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.88);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  .meta {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7);
  }

  .article {
    padding: 48px 0 96px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .shot {
    margin: 16px 0 4px;
    width: min(100%, 42rem);
  }

  .shot img {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: 20px;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .shot figcaption,
  .rates figcaption,
  .tracks figcaption {
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  blockquote {
    margin: 20px 0;
    padding: 4px 0 4px 18px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--blue);
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  blockquote p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  blockquote cite {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .prose sup a {
    color: var(--blue-deep);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .rates {
    margin: 20px 0 8px;
    width: min(100%, 42rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .rate {
    background: var(--band);
    border-radius: 20px;
    padding: 20px 12px 16px;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .rate--low {
    background: #eef4fb;
  }

  .rate__n {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 2.4rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .rate__unit,
  .rate__label {
    margin: 6px 0 0;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .rates figcaption {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }

  .tracks {
    margin: 20px 0 8px;
    width: min(100%, 42rem);
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
    padding: 20px 20px 16px;
    background: var(--band);
    border-radius: 20px;
  }

  .tracks__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .track {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .track__label {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .track svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: 36px;
    color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.12);
  }

  .track__note {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .sources {
    margin: 8px 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    max-width: 42rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .sources a {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: 24px;
    max-width: 42rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .back {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .back button {
    color: var(--blue-deep);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .rates {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
